  "answer": "The indexed arXiv research papers in the corpus do not contain data regarding the boiling point of water on Mount Everest [C1, C2].\n\nAccording to current web sources retrieved on October 27, 2026:\n\n* At the summit of Mount Everest (elevation 8,848 m / 29,029 ft), atmospheric pressure drops to approximately one-third (about 34%) of sea-level pressure, or roughly 34 kPa (255 Torr) [W2, W4].\n* Under this significantly reduced atmospheric pressure, water boils at approximately 71 °C (160 °F) based on thermodynamic barometric records [W4]. \n* Other sources place the boiling point in the range of approximately 68 °C (154 °F) [W1, W3] to around 72 °C [W2]."
